WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.460
Your nose hands the brain an orderly map, and the cortex scatters it.

2
00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:09.460
So what does the cortex keep?

3
00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:13.830
A smell starts with molecules binding receptors in the nose.

4
00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:17.490
Each receptor neuron makes one type of receptor.

5
00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:23.970
Neurons of one type send their axons to the same small knot of synapses in the bulb.

6
00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:26.170
That knot is a glomerulus.

7
00:00:27.183 --> 00:00:31.543
In mice, each receptor type usually has two glomeruli in each bulb.

8
00:00:32.533 --> 00:00:35.493
An odour binds some receptor types and not others.

9
00:00:36.233 --> 00:00:38.153
So it activates a set of glomeruli.

10
00:00:39.167 --> 00:00:40.307
Here is a question.

11
00:00:41.307 --> 00:00:43.127
Raise the concentration of the same odour.

12
00:00:43.127 --> 00:00:46.387
Do the same glomeruli light, or more of them?

13
00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:58.290
Usually more. Higher concentrations often recruit extra glomeruli, though this can

14
00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:02.590
level off. So the set is characteristic at a given concentration.

15
00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:06.390
The layout is a map of receptor identity.

16
00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:10.170
Where glomerulus sits tells you which receptor feeds it.

17
00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:13.870
The layout is only coarsely related to chemistry.

18
00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:16.430
So it is not a fine chemical map.

19
00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:20.230
Mitral and tufted cells carry the pattern out.

20
00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:23.890
Each sends one main dendrite into one glomerulus.

21
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:29.500
Cells that send their dendrites into the same glomerulus are called sister cells.

22
00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:31.440
They share one input.

23
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.060
Inhibitory interneurons sit around them.

24
00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.600
Periglomerular cells work in the glomerular layer.

25
00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.140
Granule cells have no axons.

26
00:01:42.133 --> 00:01:48.333
Mitral and tufted axons run on to piriform cortex, the main olfactory area of the

27
00:01:48.333 --> 00:01:51.233
cortex. There is no relay in the thalamus.

28
00:01:52.233 --> 00:01:54.133
There the tidy map seems to vanish.

29
00:01:55.013 --> 00:01:58.093
One odour switches on neurons scattered across the cortex.

30
00:01:59.183 --> 00:02:00.703
That raises four puzzles.

31
00:02:01.643 --> 00:02:04.803
Does the output of one glomerulus stay together in the cortex?

32
00:02:05.983 --> 00:02:10.403
If it spreads out, how can the cortex still carry which odours are chemically alike?

33
00:02:11.467 --> 00:02:13.847
Can a scattered pattern acquire learned meaning?

34
00:02:14.707 --> 00:02:17.267
And does an odour's pattern stay put from week to week?

35
00:02:18.217 --> 00:02:20.397
One idea answers the second puzzle.

36
00:02:20.397 --> 00:02:24.817
Where a piriform neuron sits tells you little about which odours excite it.

37
00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:30.900
In our simple model, its glomerular inputs and its threshold decide its response.

38
00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:36.820
Real tuning also depends on input strengths, timing and the cortex's own network.

39
00:02:37.833 --> 00:02:41.233
Each neuron gets input from several scattered glomeruli.

40
00:02:42.113 --> 00:02:44.273
It fires only when enough are active together.

41
00:02:45.317 --> 00:02:50.157
So the more glomeruli two odours share, the more neurons they share, wherever those

42
00:02:50.157 --> 00:02:51.157
neurons sit.

43
00:02:51.867 --> 00:02:56.867
With sparse responses, random wiring like this keeps the order of similarity on

44
00:02:56.867 --> 00:03:00.827
average. But related odours come out less alike than in the bulb.

45
00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:04.810
Anything tighter is not explained by this model.

46
00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:08.710
The cortex's own network and experience are the candidates.

47
00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:12.760
So the bulb sends a map of which receptors are active.

48
00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:16.240
The rest of this story is what the cortex keeps of it.
